{
  "title": "Batched games record no seed: write_game_meta reads \
MAGEBENCH_GAME_SEED, the batch launcher sets MAGEBENCH_GAME_SEEDS",
  "description": "`write_game_meta` (game_finalization.py) stamps the seed from \
the env var `MAGEBENCH_GAME_SEED`, singular. The sequential-batch path sets \
`MAGEBENCH_GAME_SEEDS`, plural — a comma-separated list, one per game. The \
singular name is never set on that path, so every batched game writes a \
game_meta.json with NO seed field at all.\n\n\
MEASURED: 0 of 166 game_meta.json files across two runs (a 20-game smoke and a \
146-game corpus run) carry a `seed` key. Both ran through the batch launcher.\n\n\
Why it matters more than a missing label. The seed is the independent variable: \
two nodes generate a shared corpus from disjoint seed blocks, and \"no game was \
generated twice\" is only checkable if each game says which seed produced it. \
Without it the property is true by construction and unprovable from the \
artefacts. It also makes any individual game unreproducible — you cannot re-run \
the one that behaved strangely.\n\n\
The obvious fallback does NOT work. `config` looks like a per-game label \
(`g915076`, written by the launcher as g<seed>.json) but it is a WORKER label: \
it repeats once per game in the batch, 5x in our runs. Recovering the seed from \
it is wrong and looks right, which is the dangerous kind of wrong.\n\n\
The file's own comment argues this case already, about temperature and arm: \
\"for a common-random-numbers experiment the seed IS the independent variable: \
if one game fails and an arm comes up short, arms misalign silently and nothing \
detects it.\" The reasoning is right; the batch path just does not reach it.\n\n\
Repro: run any batch with MAGEBENCH_GAME_SEEDS set and grep the produced \
game_meta.json for \"seed\". Expected one per game; actual none.\n\n\
Suggested fix: stamp the seed the game actually used, taken from wherever the \
batch path consumes the seed list, rather than from an env var read once per \
process. A per-process env var cannot describe a per-game value — that mismatch \
is the bug, not the variable name.",
